  "raw_text": "Josiah Sutton \u2014 wrongful conviction and DNA exoneration (Houston, Texas)\n\nOn October 25, 1998, a woman was abducted at gunpoint, raped by two men, and abandoned in a field. Five days later, while driving through her neighborhood, she identified Josiah Sutton and a friend as possible attackers.\n\nPolice obtained blood and saliva samples from both teenagers. The testing excluded Sutton's friend but suggested Sutton may have been involved. Although the victim had identified both boys, only Sutton proceeded to trial.\n\nAt the January 1999 trial, DNA evidence became the primary prosecution testimony. The Houston police crime lab claimed that semen recovered from the backseat of the car contained two profiles \u2014 Sutton's and an unidentified man's. A lab employee testified that the DNA on the victim was an \"exact match\" with Sutton, claiming that only one person in 694,000 could match. The actual figure was closer to one in sixteen Black men. Sutton received a 25-year sentence.\n\nPhysical evidence contradicted the case. Police had sought a suspect who was 5 feet 7 inches and 135 pounds; Sutton was 6 feet tall and 200 pounds. His attorney refused to pursue independent testing, claiming there were insufficient funds. The Houston lab was later shown to be deeply flawed \u2014 a criminology professor reviewing its work said the reports were \"not as scientifically sound as a decent junior high school science project.\"\n\nOn May 14, 2004, independent retesting revealed that the semen came from a single person, not two, and definitively excluded Sutton. He had served roughly five years before his release.",
